# LF in the repository and LF in the working tree, on every machine.
#
# Without this, git on Windows checks files out with CRLF (core.autocrlf), and
# anything that matches a multi-line pattern against a source file breaks the
# moment git has touched that file — which cost a mutation run here: the first
# pass worked on freshly written LF files, `git checkout --` restored them as
# CRLF, and half the patterns silently stopped matching on the second pass.
#
# Nothing in the toolchain wants CRLF. It also stops the "LF will be replaced
# by CRLF" warning on every single `git add`.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Never touched: binary, whatever the extension table thinks.
*.png binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
